--TEST--
Core\RateLimit::shed decides from this process's own memory, with no store configured, and every caller in the process charges the same count — `rule:core-classes/ratelimit-two-members` and `rule:core-classes/ratelimit-gcra`
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `spawn script` asks for `script.spawn` at its own door. `shed` itself is
# granted nothing, which is the point of the case.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true
--FILE child.nvs--
<?nvs
// Its own isolate, as a later request on any core of this process would be. The
// count it reads is the parent's: two arrivals have already used the burst.
var $late = Core\RateLimit::shed("checkout", 2, 1h);
echo "another isolate in this process: ", $late->allowed() ? "admitted" : "refused", "\n";
return "done";
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/ratelimit-two-members`'s second job: dropping load to keep a host up, where an
// approximate answer is adequate and the state is this process's own memory. No
// `[cache.shared] url` is configured here and none is needed — which is the
// whole difference from `consume`, and the reason this member declares no
// capability at all.
//
// Two per hour, so nothing measurable drains between these three lines: the
// burst defaults to the limit, which admits a period's worth at once.
var $first = Core\RateLimit::shed("checkout", 2, 1h);
var $second = Core\RateLimit::shed("checkout", 2, 1h);
var $third = Core\RateLimit::shed("checkout", 2, 1h);

if ($first->allowed() && $second->allowed()) {
    echo "two admitted", "\n";
} else {
    echo "the burst is not the limit", "\n";
}

if ($third->allowed()) {
    echo "a third admitted past the burst", "\n";
} else {
    echo "third refused", "\n";
}

// `rule:core-classes/ratelimit-gcra`: a refusal carries the exact wait, computed rather than
// estimated — half an hour, because one of the two units drains in half the
// period. Not "sometime in the next hour", which is the answer that makes every
// refused client come back in the same instant. Rounded up for the same reason
// `examples/cache.nvs` rounds up: the third call arrives a moment after the
// first, so the exact wait is a whisker under.
var $wait = $third->retryAfter();
if ($wait == null) {
    echo "refused with no wait", "\n";
} else {
    var $seconds = $wait->toSeconds();
    if ($wait->toMilliseconds() % 1000 != 0) {
        $seconds = $seconds + 1;
    }
    echo "retryAfter=", $seconds, "\n";
}

// The key is what the allowance is per, here as everywhere: another key has its
// own arrival time, and the two do not share one.
var $other = Core\RateLimit::shed("signup", 2, 1h);
if ($other->allowed()) {
    echo "another key admitted", "\n";
} else {
    echo "one key's arrivals charged another", "\n";
}

// The count is the process's and not the caller's: another isolate, which may
// run on another core, reads the arrivals this one made and is refused too.
var $job = spawn script "child.nvs" with(output: "capture");
var $done = await $job;
echo $done->output;
--EXPECT--
two admitted
third refused
retryAfter=1800
another key admitted
another isolate in this process: refused
